WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.560
"The Witcher" nods to The Conjunction&nbsp;
of the Spheres, Ithlinne's prophecy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.060
and where Witchers come from, but doesn't&nbsp;
explain any of it. The new prequel series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:14.746
"Blood Origin" answers many of those questions&nbsp;
and raises more. Warning: Spoilers ahead.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:19.680
In the time of "The Witcher," the Continent&nbsp;
is a hodgepodge of social, political,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.740
and religious groups, subject to an endless cycle&nbsp;
of war and racial conflict. Much of that strife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.380
comes as a result of human machinations, as the&nbsp;
many human nations of the Continent are prone to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.840
infighting just as often as they are to prejudice&nbsp;
against Elves, Dwarves, and the like. One of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:42.660
setting's most enduring and poignant features is&nbsp;
the abundance of Elven ruins and artifacts across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:48.180
the land, over which humanity builds their empires&nbsp;
and attempts to recreate old Elvish magics.

00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:53.994
"My elders worked with humans,&nbsp;
and got robbed of all they had.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.864 --> 00:00:57.480
And when they fought back, they were slaughtered."

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:03.000
Yet in "The Witcher: Blood Origin," humanity has&nbsp;
yet to arrive on the Continent, and in its place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.000
the Elven empires prove themselves every bit as&nbsp;
bloodthirsty as their eventual human successors.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.160
The series begins with three major&nbsp;
Elven kingdoms uniting under one banner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.260
with Xin'trea as its capital city. The&nbsp;
unification is no feat of diplomacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:24.060
but rather the act of a few scheming,&nbsp;
power-mad individuals, specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:29.700
Balor and Eredin. The new, unified empire&nbsp;
is built on a shaky foundation as a result.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:36.900
This period is known in canon as a golden age in&nbsp;
Elven history, and yet even then it was a violent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:42.720
tenuous time. By the series' end, the death of an&nbsp;
empress, the shattering of dimensional barriers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.340
and a peasant uprising all bring the&nbsp;
so-called golden age to an ugly end.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:53.880
Though they've yet to properly arrive in "The&nbsp;
Witcher" to hound Ciri and plague Geralt's dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.860
the Wild Hunt is one of the greatest threats&nbsp;
in the Witcher canon and is likely to end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.540
as the series' overarching villains.&nbsp;
In the Sapkowski novels, video games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:09.120
and other media, the Wild Hunt is one of the&nbsp;
primary groups responsible for Ciri's constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.340
pursuit and nomadic lifestyle. They want Ciri's&nbsp;
unique power that comes from her Elder Blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.120
a power that is prophesied to&nbsp;
either end the world or save it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.320
All of their efforts begin in the final&nbsp;
moments of "The Witcher: Blood Origin."

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.060
When Balor betrays Eredin and casts&nbsp;
him out into an unknown dimension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:33.060
the Elf seems doomed to a slow demise on a&nbsp;
dead, alien world. But it's precisely that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:38.880
exile that causes Eredin, alongside his band&nbsp;
of loyal soldiers, to begin transforming into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:44.520
the fearsome Wild Hunt. Eredin's singular&nbsp;
determination allows him to survive exile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:50.520
discover the secrets to interdimensional travel,&nbsp;
and return to the Continent as the King of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:56.760
Wild Hunt. Using advanced magic found only on&nbsp;
other worlds, Eredin and his band evolve into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:02.040
something more than simple raiders. Wielding&nbsp;
frost magic and commanding elemental beasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:07.380
they become terrifying figures who haunt the&nbsp;
dreams of folk from the Continent and beyond.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:13.800
"The Witcher: Blood Origin" establishes the origin&nbsp;
for several major moments in the franchise's lore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:19.140
the biggest and most important are the Conjunction&nbsp;
and the creation of the first Witcher. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:24.300
the series also finds time to sprinkle in the&nbsp;
origins of smaller characters that are destined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:30.780
as more hardcore fans will undoubtedly know, to&nbsp;
become central figures in Witcher lore. One such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:36.780
figure is Ithlinne, who appears in the series for&nbsp;
just a few short scenes as a poor little girl who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:42.600
happens to have psychic visions. As it turns out,&nbsp;
that little girl eventually grows into the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.940
famous seer in history whose most famous prophecy&nbsp;
points to Ciri as the cause of Armageddon.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.300
In the final moments of&nbsp;
the series, Ithlinne offers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:56.520
Éile the Lark a prophecy regarding her child&nbsp;
and its descendants. As ominous as its sounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:02.400
it's nothing compared to what she'll predict just&nbsp;
a few years later. Unlike so many other oracles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:08.760
an alarming amount of Ithlinne's prophecies end&nbsp;
up coming true, and so her writings become almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:15.000
sacred texts in the world of the Witcher. Many&nbsp;
of the major decisions made by kings, queens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:21.043
and mages in the time of Geralt come as a result&nbsp;
of Ithlinne's words and their ever-present doom.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.400
Another minor character in "The Witcher: Blood&nbsp;
Origin" who closes out the series in an impactful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.800
way is Avallac'h. For the vast majority of&nbsp;
the series, the young Elf is merely a simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.840
mage's apprentice, a stuttering lackey with&nbsp;
no discernible importance beyond his meager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:42.420
station. His surprising rise to prominence&nbsp;
comes after saving Empress Merwyn from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:47.880
would-be assassin. Merwyn has no idea what&nbsp;
events she's setting in motion by promoting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.540
Avallac'h and granting him access&nbsp;
to higher-level magical research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:56.534
nor how he will one day play a major&nbsp;
part in the story of Geralt and Ciri.

00:04:56.534 --> 00:04:59.820
"I need you to learn how to&nbsp;
open gateways to new worlds."

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:02.921
"This is insane. I'm going to die."

00:05:02.921 --> 00:05:08.220
As fans of the novels and video games will&nbsp;
know, Avallac'h is an ancient Elven mage with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:13.560
an unparalleled knowledge of interdimensional&nbsp;
travel who eventually becomes Ciri's mentor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:18.360
bodyguard. In the game, "The Witcher III,"&nbsp;
it's thanks to Avallac'h that Ciri is able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:22.920
to evade the Wild Hunt for so long, and it's&nbsp;
only through him that she's able to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.660
the truth of her powers and finally master&nbsp;
them. By awakening Avallac'h's potential,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:32.820
Empress Merwyn unknowingly sets in motion&nbsp;
a series of events that culminate in Ciri's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:37.920
survival and therefore enable Ciri to play&nbsp;
whatever cataclysmic role she's meant to.

00:05:38.401 --> 00:05:43.020
The world of "The Witcher: Blood Origin" seems&nbsp;
harsh and violent enough for the series' first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:48.900
few episodes, but its finale takes everything&nbsp;
to an extreme new level by ushering in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:53.700
Conjunction of the Spheres. The Conjunction&nbsp;
is perhaps the single most important event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:58.740
in the Witcher canon, and even when shown in&nbsp;
the series, its full importance goes untold.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:04.740
The finale shows the Conjunction's first moments,&nbsp;
describing it as a shattering of space and time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:09.840
a tearing of the veils between worlds, but even&nbsp;
that grandiose narration doesn't begin to cover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:15.660
it. Almost instantly, the Conjunction transforms&nbsp;
the Continent from a place of relative uniformity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:21.300
and stability into a terrifying, savage land&nbsp;
where dozens to hundreds of different species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:26.460
of monsters, humans and elves included,&nbsp;
war with each other for supremacy. Gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:31.020
are the days when a peasant's greatest&nbsp;
concerns were taxes and blight. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:38.400
they are replaced with the existential terror of&nbsp;
vampires, basilisks, kikimores, and leshys. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:44.040
establishment of the various Witcher schools is a&nbsp;
direct response to this sudden influx of monstrous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:48.600
beasts and without the Conjunction, there would&nbsp;
be no Witchers around which to build a franchise.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:54.540
As any longtime fan of the "Witcher" franchise&nbsp;
can tell you, the real monsters aren't the beings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:59.220
with sharp claws, stingers, and rotting&nbsp;
flesh, they're the very people around us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:04.200
the regular citizens who suddenly resort to&nbsp;
ghoulish acts of violence and selfishness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:09.840
That humanity is the real monster, is one of&nbsp;
the central themes that guide every aspect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:15.000
"The Witcher" franchise. In a clever stroke of&nbsp;
world-building, Sapkowski chose to introduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:20.700
humans to the Continent at the same time as the&nbsp;
more obvious monsters, making the creation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:25.680
the Witchers a dual necessity. In the latter&nbsp;
half of "The Witcher: Blood Origin" finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:31.260
the Conjunction of the Spheres brings humanity&nbsp;
to the Continent, forever changing its landscape.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:37.020
In the finale, an Elven fisherman stumbles&nbsp;
upon a shipwreck whose passengers speak no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:43.260
Elvish and bear smooth, rounded ears. Those&nbsp;
first human settlers, though unintentionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:49.200
start a process on the Continent that in many ways&nbsp;
resembles an infection, spreading from nation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.355
nation, tearing them down to continue&nbsp;
feeding and increasing their numbers.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:59.040
One of the more curious new additions to the&nbsp;
Witcher lore in Netflix's "The Witcher" is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:04.740
presence of the monoliths, strange, black pillars&nbsp;
of stone that harbor deep secrets relating to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:09.900
magic and the Conjunction of the Spheres. The&nbsp;
video games have their own monoliths which seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:14.820
unrelated, and the books have no mention of them&nbsp;
at all, making them a novel invention for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:20.160
Netflix series. They pop up again and again in&nbsp;
"The Witcher," forming the basis of Istredd's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:25.920
research, interfering with Ciri's power, and&nbsp;
even summoning several entirely new species of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:31.680
monsters. The mysterious structures return in "The&nbsp;
Witcher: Blood Origin," and like other important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.600
facets of the universe, their true nature and&nbsp;
origin are revealed more fully in the series.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:42.780
It turns out that the monoliths are Dwarven&nbsp;
creations, only controlled by the Elves because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:48.180
their conquest of the Dwarves and their territory.&nbsp;
Their magic is ancient, predating even Elvish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:53.760
magic, and is intrinsically tied to the land and&nbsp;
the underlying properties of the dimension itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:59.220
Just as the monoliths withstood the magical&nbsp;
experimentation of the Elves and endured until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:04.980
the time of humanity, they also endured throughout&nbsp;
the days of Dwarven dominion and Elvish conquest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:09.636
making them some of the only unchanging&nbsp;
features of the Continent's landscape.

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:14.280
The world of the Witcher is chock full&nbsp;
of fascinating characters, creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:19.320
coalitions, and conflicts that make for excellent&nbsp;
story material, but at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:25.320
the reason we're here is the Witcher himself,&nbsp;
Geralt of Rivia. He and his fellow cat-eyed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:30.840
sword-slinging brethren give the franchise its&nbsp;
namesake and are Sapkowski's primary invention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:35.520
"The Witcher: Blood Origin" is fully aware&nbsp;
of this fact, which is why much of its story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.260
is dedicated to the creation, and eventual&nbsp;
destruction, of the Continent's first Witcher.

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:44.640
The first Witcher wasn't created by any&nbsp;
organization in response to encroaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:48.720
monsters, and it even predates the&nbsp;
Conjunction of the Spheres. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:53.700
the first Witcher is made for killing one&nbsp;
single monster. Although the process of mutating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:58.680
young boys into Witchers eventually becomes&nbsp;
standardized, the first attempt is anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:04.860
but. Fjall is laid on the dirt floor of a cave,&nbsp;
given a mix of herbs, and infused with the blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:10.320
of the most convenient monster. The transformation&nbsp;
ultimately consumes him and leads to his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:14.580
and how the process was revived and&nbsp;
eventually streamlined is still unknown.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:19.440
Bookending "The Witcher: Blood Origin"&nbsp;
are prophecies from the young Ithlinne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:24.000
The first prophecy centers around the Lark&nbsp;
and her quest, but the second prophecy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.680
the one that comes at the tail end of the&nbsp;
finale, seems tailor-made for Geralt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:33.660
Ciri. In particular, one invocation&nbsp;
of a seed bears a great portent.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:40.000
"Carry forth the first note of&nbsp;
a song that ends all times."

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.560
This is almost certainly a direct&nbsp;
reference to Ciri's Elder Blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:46.140
and her eventual role in the ending of the world.

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:51.900
This final prophecy of the series echoes the most&nbsp;
famous of Ithlinne's predictions in the books and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:57.780
games. Known in Geralt's time as simply Ithlinne's&nbsp;
Prophecy, it foretells the ending of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:02.880
all centered around Elder Blood. Elder&nbsp;
Blood flows through Ciri, which makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:07.740
the girl perhaps the greatest asset to anyone&nbsp;
in the Spheres who has a stake in Armageddon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:12.960
Like Ithlinne's Prophecy, this prediction from&nbsp;
the "Blood Origin" is likely what will set in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:18.180
motion the series of events that lead to future&nbsp;
generations' fear of those with Elder Blood.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:24.420
As "The Witcher: Blood Origin" concludes,&nbsp;
Seanchaí implores Jaskier to "sing the Song&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:30.120
of the Seven," referring to the tale of Fjall,&nbsp;
the Lark, and their five companions. She wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:34.800
the famous bard to spread the tale far and&nbsp;
wide, rekindling the fire of hope among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:39.420
Elvendom that the Lark and company first&nbsp;
breathed to life over a millennium ago.

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:44.400
Tasked by the mysterious and godlike&nbsp;
Seanchaí to sing the Song of the Seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:48.720
across the Continent, Jaskier emerges&nbsp;
from the series with a new purpose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:53.520
one meant to tip the scales of power&nbsp;
toward the side of Elves. Seanchaí's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:58.140
nature and ultimate motivations are unclear,&nbsp;
so it's difficult to determine exactly why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:02.220
she brought this tale back to life or&nbsp;
what will come from it, but regardless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:06.960
it will certainly mean more for Jaskier,&nbsp;
the sect of elves known as the Scoia'tael,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:11.820
and therefore the state of the world for Geralt&nbsp;
and Ciri, in future seasons of "The Witcher."

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:16.440
Although the majority of "The Witcher:&nbsp;
Blood Origin" is set long before the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:20.820
of Geralt and Ciri, there are two&nbsp;
brief sequences set in their era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:25.800
one at the series' very start and one at&nbsp;
its end. In those few moments, we're able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:30.000
to learn a little about the current state of&nbsp;
the Continent and its warring inhabitants.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.920
For one thing, we know that the wars in&nbsp;
the Northern Continent have for some reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:40.380
attracted attention from extra-dimensional&nbsp;
beings, hence Seanchaí's involvement. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:45.720
also confirmed that Seanchaí will be in more&nbsp;
"Witcher" projects going forward. Both she and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:50.580
the Song of the Seven are likely to appear in&nbsp;
"The Witcher" in future seasons. In addition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:55.860
the scene in which we first find Jaskier says&nbsp;
a lot about the current state of things. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.860 --> 00:13:00.720
is apparently regarded as a folk hero,&nbsp;
the noble Sandpiper, Rescuer of Elves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:05.400
and is even beloved enough to warrant his&nbsp;
rescue from human capture by an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:11.340
company of Scoia'tael. It seems since we last&nbsp;
left Jaskier and the rest of our heroes in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:16.740
Witcher" Season 2, the Continent has moved&nbsp;
forward, elevating Jaskier along with it.

